window.COURSE = {"lessons":["# 01 - Meet the cluster\n\nPrerequisite: SETUP.md complete. Goal: distinguish cluster, node, Pod and Deployment.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Draw browser -> Service -> Pod -> container. Identify the control plane.\n2. Create the namespace; apply the starting application:\n\n```powershell\nkubectl --context kind-k8training create namespace k8training\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training apply -f training/manifests/config.yaml -f training/manifests/web.yaml\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training get deployments,replicasets,pods -o wide\npowershell -NoProfile -File training/scripts/Check.ps1 -Lab 1\n```\n\n3. Inspect one Pod with describe. Locate its image, node, events and owner.\n\nPass manually: both explain desired state vs observed state and why the\nDeployment created a ReplicaSet. Record two Pod names and the node name.\nBreak/fix: request a nonexistent Pod with get; interpret NotFound and list\nthe real names. Re-run this lab by applying the two files; create namespace\nonly if absent.\nNext: 02.md.\n","# 02 - Scaling and self-healing\n\nPrerequisite: 01. Goal: predict a controller's response.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Scale web to three replicas:\n   `kubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training scale deployment/web --replicas=3`.\n2. List Pods. Record a Pod's name and UID, delete that one Pod using its name,\n   and watch with `get pods -w` (Ctrl+C stops watching).\n3. Confirm the replacement has a new UID and web returns to three Ready Pods.\n4. Run Check.ps1 -Lab 2.\n\nPass manually: retain before/after UIDs; explain why deleting a Pod does not\nscale down a Deployment. Discuss why three Pods on one node do not survive\nloss of that node.\nBreak/fix: scale to zero; predict availability; restore to three.\nReset: repeat scale --replicas=3. Applying web.yaml resets replicas to two.\nNext: 03.md.\n","# 03 - Services, selectors and DNS\n\nPrerequisite: 02. Goal: separate Pod identity from stable service identity.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Before opening service.yaml, write a ClusterIP Service named web with selector\n   app=web, port 80 and targetPort 80. Compare with the reference and apply it:\n   `kubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training apply -f training/manifests/service.yaml`.\n2. Run Check.ps1 -Lab 3. It performs a DNS/HTTP request from a temporary Pod.\n3. Run `kubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training port-forward service/web 8080:80`.\n   Open http://localhost:8080; stop forwarding with Ctrl+C.\n4. Inspect `get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web`.\n\nBreak/fix: edit the live Service selector with\n`kubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training edit service web`;\nset app to missing. Observe EndpointSlices and failed internal access.\nRestore by applying service.yaml, then repeat the check.\nPass manually: explain selector vs targetPort, show internal HTTP succeeds\nafter repair, and explain why ClusterIP is not a public internet address.\nNext: 04.md.\n","# 04 - Configuration and practice Secrets\n\nPrerequisite: 03. Goal: distinguish configuration from an image.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Edit config.yaml content to include BOTH learner first names; apply it.\n2. Predict whether the image changes. Restart web to avoid waiting for volume\n   propagation:\n   `kubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training rollout restart deployment/web`.\n3. Use the HTTP check from lab 03 to inspect the page and confirm both names.\n4. Create a fake-only Secret:\n\n```powershell\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training create secret generic practice --from-literal=token=not-a-real-secret\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training get secret practice\npowershell -NoProfile -File training/scripts/Check.ps1 -Lab 4\n```\n\nIf practice already exists, reuse it. Do not use a real token.\nBreak/fix: remove web-content with delete configmap web-content, restart web,\nand inspect events on the new Pods. Restore config.yaml and wait for rollout.\nPass manually: both names visible over HTTP; image unchanged; explain why base64\nis not encryption and why a Secret requires access controls. This Secret is\ndeliberately not consumed by the app; stretch task: inject it as an environment\nvariable into a disposable Pod without printing the value.\nNext: 05.md.\n","# 05 - Health and safe rollout\n\nPrerequisite: 04. Goal: diagnose instead of repeatedly restarting.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Inspect web.yaml. Explain requests vs limits and readiness vs liveness.\n2. Break a rollout:\n\n```powershell\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training set image deployment/web web=nginx:this-tag-does-not-exist\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training rollout status deployment/web --timeout=30s\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training get pods\n```\n\n3. Describe a failing Pod. Record the image-pull event and compare Ready old Pods.\n4. Repair with `kubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training rollout undo deployment/web`.\n5. Wait for rollout, then run Check.ps1 -Lab 5.\n\nSecond experiment: edit deployment/web and change readinessProbe.httpGet.path\nto /missing. Inspect Ready counts and events, then restore the path to /.\nPass manually: capture both failure causes, explain why readiness failure does\nnot itself restart a container, and show the recovered rollout.\nReset if undo history is confusing: apply web.yaml (replicas returns to two).\nNext: 06.md.\n","# 06 - Storage and batch work\n\nPrerequisite: 05; default StorageClass supplied by kind.\nGoal: distinguish Pod lifetime from volume lifetime.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Read storage.yaml. Predict the writer Job's lifecycle and PVC binding.\n2. Apply it:\n\n```powershell\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training apply -f training/manifests/storage.yaml\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training wait --for=condition=complete job/write-journal --timeout=120s\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training logs job/write-journal\npowershell -NoProfile -File training/scripts/Check.ps1 -Lab 6\n```\n\n3. Delete journal-reader, reapply storage.yaml, rerun validation. The note survives.\n4. Inspect get pvc,pv and get storageclass (PV and StorageClass are cluster scoped).\n\nPass manually: record PVC name and data before/after reader replacement.\nExplain why this single-node local storage is not a backup or proof of\nmulti-node failover. ReadWriteOnce allows mounting on one node, not only one Pod.\nBreak/fix: diagnose Pending PVC if no default StorageClass exists; inspect\ndescribe pvc journal and restore the intended kind environment.\nTo rerun the writer, delete job write-journal and reapply; it overwrites the\npractice note. Deleting the namespace destroys this practice data.\nNext: 07.md.\n","# 07 - Least privilege\n\nPrerequisite: 06. Goal: prove an identity's permissions.\n\nTasks:\n\n1. Write a Role granting only get/list Pods, and bind it to ServiceAccount\n   observer. Compare with rbac.yaml and apply the reference.\n2. Run Check.ps1 -Lab 7. It impersonates observer to check permissions.\n3. Test explicitly:\n\n```powershell\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:k8training:observer\nkubectl --context kind-k8training -n k8training get secrets --as=system:serviceaccount:k8training:observer\n```\n\nThe first must succeed, the second must be Forbidden.\n\nBreak/fix: delete rolebinding observer-pods; verify the allowed operation is\ndenied; restore rbac.yaml and rerun.\nPass manually: explain Role vs RoleBinding vs ServiceAccount, record allow/deny\noutput, and explain why a Pod does not automatically use observer just because\nthat account exists. Impersonation works here because kind's lab user is admin.\nDo not grant cluster-admin as a repair.\nNext: 08.md.\n","# 08 - Incident capstone\n\nPrerequisite: 01-07 complete. Goal: recover an app using evidence.\nTimebox: 45 minutes. Driver diagnoses; navigator keeps an incident timeline.\n\nBaseline: apply config.yaml, web.yaml and service.yaml. Wait for rollout.\nKeep the Secret, PVC and RBAC from earlier labs. Then inject these lab-only faults:\n\n1. Set web's image to nginx:this-tag-does-not-exist (command in lab 05).\n2. Edit Service web: selector app=missing.\n3. Edit Deployment web: readinessProbe.httpGet.path=/missing.\n\nDiagnose using get, describe, logs, rollout status and EndpointSlices.\nRecord a hypothesis and supporting observation before each repair.\nRepair each fault with targeted edits/commands; do not delete the namespace.\nUse a second terminal for port forwarding and confirm HTTP content manually.\n\nRun Check.ps1 -Lab 8 after recovery. It checks application availability, actual\nHTTP access, saved data and RBAC. It does not prove how you diagnosed the faults.\n\nPass manually:\n\n- Three causes, diagnostic evidence, repairs, and HTTP recovery recorded.\n- Both learners explain a cause and its user-visible effect without notes.\n- Explain the full browser -> port-forward -> Service/Pod path, and distinguish\n  it from an in-cluster DNS request.\n- Describe which state is lost when a Pod, namespace or whole cluster is deleted.\n- Recreate the application from files in a fresh namespace only as an optional\n  follow-up; copy the RBAC subject namespace if changing the namespace.\n\nNext learning project: package the app with Helm and add CI schema validation.\n"],"setup":"# Setup (Windows PowerShell)\n\nUse a local disposable cluster. Plan for roughly 4 free CPU cores, 8 GB free RAM\nand 15 GB disk as a comfortable starting budget, not a vendor minimum.\n\n1. Install Docker Desktop with its WSL 2 backend and Linux containers.\n   Follow https://docs.docker.com/desktop/setup/install/windows-install/ .\n   Enable virtualization/WSL if its installer requests it. Check Docker's license\n   terms for your situation. Start Docker Desktop and wait until it is ready.\n2. Install kubectl and kind, following their official pages linked in README.\n   If Windows Package Manager is available, these are installation commands:\n   `winget install --exact --id Kubernetes.kubectl`\n   `winget install --exact --id Kubernetes.kind`\n   Docker Desktop: `winget install --exact --id Docker.DockerDesktop`.\n   Installers may require elevation, WSL setup, a restart and manual acceptance.\n3. Open a fresh terminal at the repository root and verify:\n\n```powershell\ndocker version\ndocker info --format '{{.OSType}}'\nkubectl version --client\nkind version\nkind create cluster --name k8training --wait 120s\nkubectl --context kind-k8training get nodes\n```\n\nExpected: Docker server responds, OSType is linux, and one Ready node appears.\nUse a kubectl client within one minor version of the cluster API server.\nRecord versions in your evidence. If a cluster of this name already exists,\ninspect it with `kind get clusters` instead of deleting it.\nIf images cannot download, check Docker's proxy/network configuration.\n\nAll course commands explicitly use context kind-k8training. Do not change them\nto point at a work/production cluster. You do not need a cloud account.\nThe course does not install software or create the cluster automatically.\n\nBefore starting, explain: image vs container; terminal working directory; YAML\nindentation; HTTP ports. If unfamiliar, spend an extra session running\n`docker run --rm -p 8081:80 nginx:1.28.0-alpine`, opening\nhttp://localhost:8081, and stopping it with Ctrl+C.\n"};
